The '''Government of Balboa''' ({{lang|ca|Govern de Balboa}}) is the national government of the [[Balboa|Republic of Balboa]], a {{w|unitary state|unitary}} {{w|presidential republic}} in [[Abyala]]. The government is structured under the [[Constitution of Balboa|1974 constitution]], which was adopted following the country's [[Reunification of Balboa|reunification]] and establishes a separation of powers among three branches: the executive, headed by a directly elected [[President of Balboa|president]]; the legislative, consisting of a unicameral [[National Assembly of Balboa|National Assembly]]; and the judiciary, comprising the [[Constitutional Court of Balboa]], the [[Supreme Court of Balboa]], and lower courts. The constitution incorporates the [[Twelve Guarantees]] as entrenched provisions that cannot be amended or repealed without the consent of the [[Adonian Community]].

The seat of the national government is [[Sant Cristòfor]], the capital and largest city, where the president's official residence, the National Assembly building, and the principal ministries are located. The Supreme Court is seated in [[Sant Bart]], the country's administrative centre, while the Constitutional Court sits in Sant Cristòfor.

== Executive branch ==

{{main|President of Balboa|Cabinet of Balboa}}

The executive branch is headed by the president, who serves as both {{w|head of state}} and {{w|head of government}}. The president is elected by {{w|two-round system|direct popular vote}} for a five-year term and may serve a maximum of two consecutive terms. If no candidate receives an absolute majority (50%+1) in the first round, a {{w|runoff election|runoff}} is held between the top two candidates within 21 days. The vice president is elected on the same ticket as the president and assumes the presidency in the event of vacancy.

The current president is [[Lim Hui-jin]], elected in the [[2024 Balboan presidential election|2024 election]]. The current vice president is [[Adalberto Paredes]].

=== Powers of the president ===

The constitution grants the president the following powers:

* Appointing and dismissing the [[Cabinet of Balboa|cabinet]], subject to confirmation by the National Assembly
* {{w|Veto|Vetoing}} legislation, which may be overridden by a three-fifths majority of the National Assembly
* Serving as {{w|commander-in-chief}} of the [[Balboan Defence Forces]]
* Conducting foreign policy and accrediting diplomatic representatives
* Issuing regulations and executive orders within the scope of statute law
* Convening extraordinary sessions of the National Assembly

The president may not rule by decree; all executive actions must have a basis in statute or the constitution. The president may not deploy the armed forces domestically without Assembly authorisation, except in cases of direct invasion or insurrection. The president may be {{w|impeachment|impeached}} by a two-thirds majority of the National Assembly.

=== Cabinet ===

{{main|Cabinet of Balboa}}

The Cabinet of Balboa ({{lang|ca|Consell de Ministres}}) is the principal executive body, consisting of the president, the vice president, and the heads of the national ministries. Cabinet ministers are appointed by the president and must be confirmed by a majority vote of the National Assembly. The constitution requires that cabinet ministers be citizens of Balboa and that no more than half of the cabinet may be drawn from the National Assembly; ministers who are Assembly members must resign their seats upon appointment.

The cabinet is collectively responsible to the president and, through the confirmation and oversight process, to the National Assembly. Ministers may be summoned to appear before Assembly committees and are required to provide written reports on their areas of responsibility upon request.

